# Globalized bounded Nelder–Mead

Globalized bounded Nelder–Mead (GBNM) combines local simplex search with
random restarts inside a finite box. It uses objective values alone and
remembers previous search starts and possible local optima. Restart
locations favor less explored regions, while additional simplex tests
check whether a local search has collapsed or become trapped at a bound.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gbnm/struct.Gbnm.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function on a finite box |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` |
| Search | Bounded local searches with seeded global restarts |
| Constraints | A box-only declaration through `Constraints` |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider GBNM when derivatives are unavailable, several local solutions
may exist, and bounded Nelder–Mead searches are a reasonable way to explore
each region. [Luersen and Le Riche (2004)](#ref-luersen-le-riche-2004)
developed the method for engineering optimization with a limited
evaluation budget.

Unlike a fresh uniform restart each time, GBNM uses its search history to
spread later starts through the box. The first local search starts around
the point you provide, after projecting it into the bounds, so you can
begin with a useful existing design.

For one local search, compare [Nelder–Mead](../../derivative-free/nelder-mead/).
[DIRECT](../direct/) gives deterministic box partitioning, while
[differential evolution](../de/) maintains a population
instead of a sequence of simplexes. On a smooth problem where a local
model is useful, compare [BOBYQA](../../derivative-free/bobyqa/).

Restarts broaden the search but do not certify global optimality in a
finite run. Evaluate the best result against the budget, several seeds,
and any known reference solution.

## How it works

With `n` free coordinates, each local search maintains `n + 1` vertices.
It uses Nelder–Mead reflection, expansion, contraction, and shrink moves,
projecting trial points into the box before evaluation. Basin starts each
search with a regular simplex whose edge length is a fraction of the
smallest free-coordinate box width.

Small-simplex, flat-cost, and degeneracy tests trigger the restart logic.
Depending on the current test and location, the solver either constructs
a small or large test simplex around the current best vertex, or makes a
probabilistic restart elsewhere. These tests help distinguish a possible
local optimum from a simplex damaged by projection or collapse. Possible
local optima are retained when the restart tests justify doing so.

For a probabilistic restart, Basin samples 10 candidate centers uniformly
by default. It selects the candidate with the lowest Parzen density
relative to previous starts and retained local optima. The Gaussian
mixture gives greater density near previously visited regions. Its
default coordinate variance is `0.01 * (upper - lower)²`.

The new simplex edge is then sampled between 2% and 10% of the smallest
free-coordinate box width. Local convergence starts another search; it
does not establish convergence of the global algorithm.

## Example

This sphere has its minimum at `[0.0, 0.0]`. The example makes the box
declaration explicit, seeds the restart generator, and runs a fixed
evaluation budget. Even on this simple objective, the result should use
the historical best: the active local search can move to a worse point
when a restart begins.

<!-- compile: solver-gbnm -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet, Constraints,
    CostFunction, Executor, Gbnm,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct BoundedSphere {
    lower: Vec<f64>,
    upper: Vec<f64>,
}

impl CostFunction for BoundedSphere {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x.iter().map(|v| v * v).sum())
    }
}

impl Constraints for BoundedSphere {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<Vec<f64>>>;

    fn constraints(&self) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
            Some(&self.lower),
            Some(&self.upper),
        ))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let problem = BoundedSphere {
        lower: vec![-5.0; 2],
        upper: vec![5.0; 2],
    };
    let solver = Gbnm::new(42);

    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![4.0, 4.0])
        .max_cost_evals(2_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-4);
    assert!(result.best_param().iter().all(|v| (-5.0..=5.0).contains(v)));
    assert!(result.best_cost() <= result.cost());
}
```

The assertions check an analytic reference and bound feasibility. Reaching
the budget is an execution limit, rather than a global convergence claim.

## Practical considerations

### Declare bounds and scale the coordinates

GBNM requires `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<V>>` with both bound vectors
present, finite, ordered, and matching the parameter dimension.
Declarations with additional constraint blocks are unsupported. Equal
endpoints pin a coordinate and remove it from simplex geometry, density
calculations, and local tests. An all-fixed box evaluates its single
feasible point once.

The smallest free-coordinate interval sets the simplex edge scale. Very
different coordinate widths can therefore produce small initial moves in
wide intervals. Rescale the problem when the natural parameter units make
this geometry unsuitable. `with_initial_simplex_fraction` defaults to
`0.05` and adjusts the first search's scale.

### Treat tolerances as restart controls

`with_normalized_simplex_size_tolerance` defaults to `1e-6`, and
`with_absolute_simplex_cost_tolerance` defaults to `1e-20`. These tests
trigger another local search. They do not stop the outer algorithm.
The edge-ratio and normalized-determinant degeneracy thresholds both
default to `1e-7` and have separate setters.

Small- and large-test simplex fractions default to `1e-5` and `0.05`.
`with_test_simplex_fractions` changes them together. The paper prescribes
their relative roles without universal numerical sizes; Basin's defaults
make the small test larger than the default local size tolerance. If you
change those scales, keep that relationship deliberate.

### Spend restarts where they help

`with_restart_candidates` changes how many possible centers the density
test compares. Candidate selection uses geometry and history, without
objective evaluations. More candidates add that work and may choose a
less explored center, but do not guarantee a better objective.
`with_gaussian_variance_factor` controls how broadly visited regions
contribute to the density.

Use `max_cost_evals`, `max_iter`, `max_time`, or `target_objective` on the
executor. One local simplex operation or restart completes before the
next budget check. For `n` free coordinates, a local step can evaluate up
to `n + 2` points, and a restart evaluates `n + 1`, so the cost budget can
be exceeded by up to `n + 1` evaluations.

### Distinguish the active search from the incumbent

`param()` and `cost()` describe the best vertex of the active local
search. They can worsen after a restart. `best_param()` and `best_cost()`
retain the historical objective incumbent across searches. Use checked
`current()` and `best()` state readers when non-finite evaluations may
leave either unavailable.

`NaN` costs sort behind comparable values, and positive infinity can
reject an unsuitable point. A completely non-finite fixed box reports
failure. Flat regions can trigger many restarts, so inspect the result
and evaluation budget before interpreting repeated local tests as useful
exploration.

## Implementation

`PointState` publishes the active local result, historical incumbent, and
raw evaluation counts. The solver owns the simplex, local Nelder–Mead
workspace, restart history, and seeded `ChaCha8Rng`. Retain it with
`Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect `vertices()`, `costs()`,
`search_starts()`, `local_optima()`, and `restart_count()`.

Fresh initialization resets the RNG to the configured seed, clears the
history, projects the supplied point, and evaluates a new regular simplex.
A state-only snapshot therefore starts a new search from its current
point. Exact solver-and-state checkpoints preserve the live simplex,
history, RNG, and evaluation counts and skip initialization. The solver
and progress support serialization with `serde`.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The
degeneracy test uses internal pure-Rust algebra, without a backend matrix
operation or BLAS/LAPACK provider. See the API's [Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gbnm/struct.Gbnm.html#backends)
for the vector, projection, and uniform-sampling capabilities required by
custom parameter types.

